
Soft Link (Symbolic Link) : A soft link is like a shortcut to another file.
=========================


df -h path   --> to know file system of provided path.


# Create symlink for file
ln -s original.txt softlink.txt


# create symlink for directory
ln -s directory  directory



# Example:
original.txt
     ↑
     |
softlink.txt


# If you run: cat softlink.txt
Linux follows the link and reads original.txt.


readlink -f symlink_file    --> to know physical path of file


ln -s /home/viswa/file1.txt(target file)  /tmp/file2.txt(link)
ls -l /tmp/file2.txt(or)folder     --> You should see something like:  lrwxrwxrwx. 1 viswa viswa 21 Oct 4 16:52 /tmp/file2.txt -> /home/viswa/file1.txt  --> it is link details
ls -lL /tmp/file2.txt(or)folder    --> You should get something like:  -rw-r--r--. 1 viswa viswa 8 Oct 4 16:53 /tmp/file2.txt                            --> it is target details
ls -ld /tmp/link_folder            --> Displays information about the link itself without listing a directory's contents.
ls -lLd /tmp/link_folder           --> Follows the link but displays the target directory's own details, not its contents.


Command	        Link to a file	        Link to a directory
==========        ==================       =======================
ls -l link	   Link details	        Link details
ls -lL link	   Target file details	   Target directory contents
ls -ld link	   Link details	        Link details
ls -lLd link	   Target file details	   Target directory details



# You might see:
-rw-r--r-- 1 user user 100 Aug 16 original.txt
lrwxrwxrwx 1 user user  12 Aug 16 softlink.txt -> original.txt

The l at the beginning means it is a symbolic link.


# To see the physical path of directory
pwd -P

pwd -P  --> to know physical directory for symbolic link
cd -P /home/viswa/logs     --> to change directory of physical directory of path /home/viswa/logs
cd -L /home/viswa/logs     --> to preserve directory Logical path



# to unlink
unlink file_name   --> removes the directory entry (name) file1. Whether the actual data becomes inaccessible/deleted depends on whether other links to the same inode exist.




Important characteristics
=========================
Can link to a file or directory
Can point to a file on a different filesystem
Has its own inode
If the original file is deleted, the soft link becomes broken
Can point to another soft link

